oxzstd is written from the Zstandard specification. It includes data from that
specification, and some numeric settings chosen to match the reference
implementation, credited here.


1. RFC 8878, "Zstandard Compression and the 'application/zstd' Media Type"
   Y. Collet and M. Kucherawy (Ed.), February 2021
   https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8878

   From the RFC's tables:

   - src/decode.rs: the literal length and match length codes, their baselines
     and extra bits (section 3.1.1.3.2.1.1), and the offset codes built from
     them; src/encode/opt.rs and src/encode/seq.rs work from the same tables
   - src/fse.rs: the predefined distributions for literal lengths, match
     lengths and offsets (section 3.1.1.3.2.2)

